# Testing Standards — CP Build Command Center

Every code change requires corresponding tests. No exceptions. This dashboard will grow
into a complex system; tests are how we guarantee it never breaks silently.

## Test Types Required

| What you're changing | Required tests |
|---|---|
| Pure utility / lib function | Unit test in `__tests__/unit/` |
| React component | Unit test (render + interaction) in `__tests__/unit/` |
| API route handler | Integration test in `__tests__/integration/` |
| Full user flow (auth, form, navigation) | E2E test in `e2e/` |
| Permission / auth logic | Unit + integration |
| New DevTools panel | Unit test for the component |

## Directory Layout

```
__tests__/
  unit/          # Vitest — isolated, no DB, no network
  integration/   # Vitest + MSW — API handlers with mocked DB
e2e/             # Playwright — real browser, running app
```

## Unit Tests (`__tests__/unit/`)

- Use `vitest` + `@testing-library/react` + `@testing-library/user-event`
- Mock all external dependencies (db, auth, fetch)
- One `describe` block per function/component
- Name: `<subject>.unit.test.ts(x)`

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — isolated, fast, no side effects
import { hasPermission } from "@/lib/permissions";
describe("hasPermission()", () => {
  it("returns false when MEMBER checks INVITE_MEMBER", () => {
    expect(hasPermission("MEMBER", "invite:member")).toBe(false);
  });
});
```

## Integration Tests (`__tests__/integration/`)

- Use MSW (`msw/node`) to mock fetch calls inside handlers
- Test the route handler logic end-to-end (request → response)
- Must test: happy path, auth failure (401), bad input (400), forbidden (403)
- Name: `<subject>.integration.test.ts`

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD — tests the full handler chain, mocked DB
import { GET } from "@/app/api/offline/preferences/route";
vi.mock("@/lib/db", () => ({ db: { offlinePreference: { findUnique: vi.fn() } } }));
```

### External-API Fixture Rule (CRITICAL)

Any API route that accepts data sourced from an external system (Unifier, Salesforce, etc.) **must** include at least one integration test fixture where each nullable field is `null`, `""`, or omitted entirely. Never let every fixture use a tidy non-empty value for a field the external system can legitimately leave blank.

Fields that Unifier can return as `null` or absent: `projectManagerName`, `clientName`, `address`, `location`, `status`, `projectPhase`, `estimatorName`, `installManagerName`, `startDate`, `fieldDueDate`.

```typescript
// ✅ REQUIRED — cover the null/empty case alongside the happy path
it("accepts project with no PM assigned (Unifier returns null)", async () => {
  const res = await POST(new Request("...", {
    body: JSON.stringify({ projectName: "X", siteLocation: "Y", projectManagerName: "", unifierPid: "1" }),
  }));
  expect(res.status).toBe(201);
});

// ❌ FORBIDDEN — every fixture uses "PM"; no test for the null case
// projectManagerName: "PM"  ← always non-empty means you never test the empty path
```

### Form Component Rule

Any React form component that submits data to an API must have a unit test that:
1. Renders with a fixture where at least one field is `null` or empty (mirroring real data)
2. Verifies the resulting API call does not fail schema validation

## E2E Tests (`e2e/`)

- Use Playwright, run against a live dev server
- Cover complete user journeys, not individual UI states
- Use `--headed` locally to watch actions in real-time
- Keep each spec focused on ONE workflow
- Name: `<workflow>.spec.ts`

## Coverage Targets

- Utilities / lib: **90%+**
- API routes: **80%+** (integration)
- Components: **70%+** (unit)
- Critical paths (auth, permissions): **100%**

## After Every Change

```bash
npm run test:unit          # fast unit suite — always run locally
npm run test:integration   # integration suite
npm run test:smoke         # smoke E2E — catches broken routes
npm run test:coverage      # full coverage report before PR
```

## Continuous Test Maintenance — Required on Every File You Touch

Whenever a file is modified (source or test), the agent must:

### 1. Audit coverage of the entire file being edited

Do not only add tests for the lines you changed. Check the existing test file for the module
and identify any exported functions, branches, or behaviours that have **no test at all**.
Add coverage for those gaps in the same PR as the source change.

Priority:
- Functions with no describe block → add a full suite
- Async functions that call external dependencies (db, session, cookies) → ensure mocks are configured and both success + failure paths are covered
- Role/permission-sensitive code paths → always test the non-permitted case

### 2. Review existing tests for accuracy

Before committing, read the existing tests in the file you are modifying and ask:
- Does this test still describe what the app actually does?
- Would this test catch a real regression, or is it just asserting implementation details?
- Are the mock return values plausible (e.g. not hardcoding values the real system would never return)?

If an existing test is stale, misleading, or tests the wrong thing — fix or remove it in the same PR.

### 3. Calibrate assumptions with Phil before writing new tests

Whenever a test scenario requires an assumption about product behaviour (e.g. "which roles can see X?", "what does an empty response look like?", "should previewing as role Y block Z?"), **ask Phil to confirm before writing the test**. Use the AskQuestion tool with concrete yes/no or multiple-choice options.

Do NOT write tests based on guesses. A test that asserts incorrect expected behaviour is worse than no test.

### 4. Flag dead-code tests honestly

If a test passes but the behaviour it is testing no longer exists (e.g. a filter that no data currently matches), add an inline comment documenting this:

```typescript
// NOTE: No current tour steps navigate to /users, so this filter is currently
// a no-op. This test documents the intent for when /users steps are added.
it("omits /users steps for roles without VIEW_DASHBOARD", ...)
```

Do not silently keep tests that assert coincidentally-passing conditions.

## Consistency Checklist (run before every commit)

- [ ] New code has matching test file
- [ ] Existing tests in touched files are still accurate (reviewed, not just passing)
- [ ] Coverage gaps in touched files are addressed or tracked
- [ ] `npm run test:unit` passes
- [ ] `npm run lint` passes  
- [ ] `npm run build` passes (no TS errors)
- [ ] No hardcoded test credentials in committed code
- [ ] Tests describe BEHAVIOUR, not implementation details
- [ ] Any assumption about expected behaviour was confirmed with Phil before asserting it in a test
